/// \defgroup grp_w2_registry_settings Install settings: gi.dat and the registry readers
/// \ingroup grp_common_services
/// \brief The per-install settings file gi.dat, its lazy getters, and the
///        two registry readers for the game language and version.
///
/// BFME 2 keeps a few per-install facts in a small data file, gi.dat.
/// They include the product name and a SKU name, the Windows registry key
/// it keeps its own settings under, the installer's registry key, the
/// online server, the name of the user-data folder, and values the
/// launcher handshake uses (object names and the expected launcher text).
/// The table below lists all ten keys the loader knows. Zero Hour has no
/// such file: its registry keys, server address, launcher names and
/// launcher text are written into the code. Each value has a getter, and
/// the first call to
/// any of them makes LoadRegistryValues read the file. Everything else
/// reads the values through the getters.
///
/// The "Registry" in the getter names is misleading: these values never
/// come from the Windows registry. One of them decides where the registry
/// reads and writes go, though. Every BFME 2 form of GetStringFromRegistry
/// and GetUnsignedIntFromRegistry roots its key path at the GameRegPath
/// value. All but one do so through buildGameRegistryPath, which inserts a
/// backslash before a subkey that does not already start with one. The
/// exception is WWDownload's `std::string` GetStringFromRegistry: it calls
/// GetRegistryGameRegPath itself and appends the subkey with no separator,
/// as Zero Hour did with its fixed key. The callers traced here,
/// FormatURLFromRegistry and the patch check's lookup of the `Proxy`
/// value, both pass an empty subkey, so the difference does not show
/// there. The readers try `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` first and
/// `HKEY_CURRENT_USER` second. GetRegistryLanguage and GetRegistryVersion
/// are two convenience readers built on them; only the language is cached.
///
/// | gi.dat key | Getter | Who reads it, among the callers traced here |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | `SkuName` | GetRegistrySkuName | not traced here |
/// | `GameName` | GetRegistryGameName | the main window's first title, built for initializeAppWindows |
/// | `GameRegPath` | GetRegistryGameRegPath | the three buildGameRegistryPath forms and WWDownload's GetStringFromRegistry, so every GetStringFromRegistry and GetUnsignedIntFromRegistry call, and WWDownload's SetStringInRegistry and SetUnsignedIntInRegistry |
/// | `InstallerRegPath` | GetRegistryInstallerRegPath | the main window's first title |
/// | `OnlineServer` | GetRegistryOnlineServer | FormatURLFromRegistry, for the patch and news URLs, and the online start-up's patch check, which parses it directly |
/// | `UserDataLeafName` | GetRegistryUserDataLeafName | the user-data folder name, as a fallback |
/// | `G1` | GetRegistryG1 | CopyProtect::isLauncherRunning: the launcher's mutex name |
/// | `G2` | GetRegistryG2 | not traced here |
/// | `G3` | GetRegistryG3 | CopyProtect::notifyLauncher: the launcher's event name |
/// | `G4` | GetRegistryG4 | CopyProtect::validate, and a second launcher-text check run from GameLogic::update: the text expected from the launcher |
///
/// \par The gi.dat format
/// LoadRegistryValues reads the whole file into memory and accepts it only
/// when the read returns as many bytes as the file holds and the first four
/// bytes are the characters `G`, `I`, space, space. Then:
/// - bytes 4 to 7 hold the number of entries, a little-endian signed 32-bit
///   integer. Zero or a negative count means no entries;
/// - the entries follow from byte 8. Each is a NUL-terminated key followed
///   by a NUL-terminated value, both 8-bit text.
///
/// Keys are matched without regard to case. Unknown keys are skipped, and
/// when a key appears twice the later value wins. Values are not copied:
/// each getter returns a pointer into the loaded block, which is never
/// freed, so the pointers stay valid until the process exits.
///
/// \par When a value is missing
/// In retail, each of the stored value pointers starts out pointing at the
/// text `GI.DAT ERROR` (a fact read from game.dat's initialised data). A
/// missing or rejected gi.dat, or a missing key, therefore gives that text
/// and never a null pointer. With no gi.dat, GetStringFromRegistry and
/// GetUnsignedIntFromRegistry would look under a key named `GI.DAT ERROR`
/// and find nothing, so every setting read through them would fall back to
/// its default, and the launcher handshake would look for objects of that
/// name.
///
/// \par Compared with Zero Hour and BFME 1
/// - Zero Hour has no gi.dat. Its Common/System registry.cpp hard-codes
///   its key paths under `SOFTWARE\Electronic Arts\EA Games` (the Zero
///   Hour key for most reads, a Generals key for
///   GetStringFromGeneralsRegistry), and its GetRegistryGameName is a
///   different function. It returns an AsciiString read from the
///   registry's `SKU` value, defaulting to `GeneralsMPTest`.
/// - Zero Hour's CopyProtection.cpp compiles in the launcher's mutex and
///   event names (GUID strings) and the text validate expects.
/// - BFME 1 has no gi.dat: its retail executable carries no gi.dat name
///   and no `GI.DAT ERROR` text, and BFME 1's own
///   %GetRegistryUserDataLeafName (a different function from the BFME 2
///   getter documented here) reads only the registry's `UserDataLeafName`
///   value.
/// - GetRegistryLanguage and GetRegistryVersion keep Zero Hour's bodies.
///   Only the key path they read under has changed.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - gi.dat is opened with the C runtime's `fopen`, not through
///   TheFileSystem. BIG archives and the `-mod` option cannot replace it; a
///   different file has to sit where the game looks for it (see
///   LoadRegistryValues).
/// - GameRegPath moves every setting read through GetStringFromRegistry and
///   GetUnsignedIntFromRegistry at once. Those settings include the
///   language, the version, the user-data folder name, the CD-key check
///   (doCDKeyAuthentication), LAN game joins (LANAPI::RequestGameJoin), the
///   online login preferences, and the download settings `BaseURL` and
///   `Proxy`. A separate install, such as a stand-alone mod, can point it
///   at its own key. It does not move the window title's `DisplayName`
///   (read under InstallerRegPath) or the library readers that open their
///   own keys, such as WWDownload's Use_Non_Blocking_Mode with its fixed
///   Westwood key.
/// - The window title that lasts comes from the string file, not from
///   gi.dat: GameName (or the installer's `DisplayName`) is only the title
///   the window is created with (see GetRegistryGameName).
/// - The registry's `UserDataLeafName` value wins over gi.dat's; gi.dat
///   only supplies the default (getUserDataLeafName and
///   getUserDataLeafNameAscii try the registry first).
/// - Among its traced uses, the registry's `Language` value picks the
///   splash image, the language BIG archives (and, with local files
///   preferred, the `lang\<language>` loose-file folder), the patch and
///   message-of-the-day URLs (see GetRegistryOnlineServer), and the online
///   login screen's default country (see GetRegistryLanguage);
///   \ref page_modding_files describes the archives and folders.
///   It does not pick the string file: that name is fixed (see
///   GetRegistryLanguage). The archives it selects can only change which
///   copy of that file the file system finds; whether the shipped language
///   archives hold one is not verified here.
/// - Most of these values are read only by start-up and front-end code.
///   G4 is the exception: GameLogic::update also checks the launcher text
///   against it at one point during a game and posts a game message when
///   the check fails (see GetRegistryG4). What that message does is not
///   traced here, so do not assume a G4 change is safe for lockstep play.
/// - The installed version is passed to the GameSpy peer SDK as the
///   title's version, so online matchmaking may depend on it (see
///   GetRegistryVersion).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - This is the seam for running without the original installer. gi.dat
///   supplies the names and the registry location. The GameRegPath reads
///   described here all end in a few small readers (getStringFromRegistry,
///   getUnsignedIntFromRegistry and their `std::string` copies in
///   WWDownload) that call `RegOpenKeyEx` and `RegQueryValueEx`. A port
///   without a registry can replace those readers, for example with a
///   settings file keyed by the same value names, and keep their callers
///   unchanged. Ship a gi.dat, or replace the getters, so that the names
///   and paths are not `GI.DAT ERROR`.
/// - The seam also writes. WWDownload's SetStringInRegistry and
///   SetUnsignedIntInRegistry root their path at GameRegPath through
///   buildGameRegistryPath, try `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` and then
///   `HKEY_CURRENT_USER`, and write through setStringInRegistry and
///   setUnsignedIntInRegistry, which create the key with `RegCreateKeyEx`.
///   The traced use is hasWriteAccess: before a queued patch download,
///   startOnline calls it. It first tests that it can create a file in the
///   current directory, then reads `Version` and writes it back. When any
///   of these three steps fails (the file creation, the read or the
///   write), the player gets the `GUI:MustHaveAdminRights` message instead
///   of the download. A registry-free port has to back these writers too.
///   Common/System has narrow setStringInRegistry and
///   setUnsignedIntInRegistry as well, whose callers are not traced here.
/// - Not every registry access is rooted at GameRegPath. The window title
///   passes the InstallerRegPath key, under `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE`, to the
///   low-level wide getStringFromRegistry, so a replacement for that reader
///   must handle that key too. The patch downloader's FTP mode check
///   (Use_Non_Blocking_Mode) opens a fixed `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` key with
///   its own `RegOpenKeyExA` call, and WWLib's RegistryClass, which
///   DX8Wrapper's render-device save and load functions use, opens a key
///   its caller names with its own calls (whether BFME 2 reaches those
///   functions is not traced here). A registry-free port has to handle
///   these separately.
/// - The launcher handshake is configured here. BFME 2's WinMain returns
///   at start-up unless CopyProtect::isLauncherRunning finds the G1 mutex
///   already created and CopyProtect::notifyLauncher reaches the launcher
///   through the G3 event. A port that starts the game without the
///   original launcher has to provide both, or change that start-up check.
///   The game logic's update repeats a G4 text check later (see
///   GetRegistryG4), and that check fails when no launcher shared memory is
///   mapped, so bypassing the start-up check alone does not cover it.
/// - The file is opened in text mode and its size is checked against the
///   bytes read. Under the Microsoft C runtime, text mode drops the CR of
///   each CR LF pair and stops at a Ctrl-Z byte. Either one anywhere in the
///   file, including in the count, makes the read come back short, and the
///   whole file is ignored. A port that opens it in binary mode would accept
///   files that retail rejects.
/// - Nothing checks the format: the count is trusted, entries are scanned
///   for their NUL without regard to the end of the block, and the
///   allocation is not checked. Only trusted files should be read. The
///   count and the magic are read as native 32-bit integers, so a
///   big-endian port must swap them.
/// - The values are narrow strings. The window-title and wide user-data
///   readers widen them, so non-ASCII values depend on that conversion.
///
/// \ref sub_common_services covers the rest of the common services.

/// \file Common/System/RegistryBlockLoader.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_registry_settings
/// \brief LoadRegistryValues, the gi.dat reader, and the directory-prefix
///        buffer it builds the file's path from.
///
/// BFME 2 only: there is no Zero Hour counterpart. The key dispatcher that
/// each entry is handed to lives in RegistryValueDispatch.cpp.
/// LoadRegistryValues is the only reader of the buffer, and a small copy
/// helper is its only writer. No reference to that helper turns up in
/// game.dat's code (see LoadRegistryValues).

/// \file Common/System/RegistryValueDispatch.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_registry_settings
/// \brief The gi.dat key dispatcher, the stored value pointers and the ten
///        lazy getters that serve them.
///
/// BFME 2 only. The dispatcher compares a key against the ten known names
/// and stores the value pointer in the matching slot. Each getter checks
/// one shared flag, calls LoadRegistryValues the first time any of them
/// runs, and returns its slot. The dispatcher is described here only by
/// its role. GetRegistryG2 is not documented, because this page does not
/// trace what its value is for.

/// \file Common/System/RegistryLanguage.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_registry_settings
/// \brief GetRegistryLanguage, the cached registry reader for the game's
///        language name.
///
/// Zero Hour's body from Common/System/registry.cpp, ported through BFME 1,
/// in a unit of its own. The unit also holds the compiler-generated
/// exit-time destructor of the cached string.

/// \file Common/System/RegistryVersion.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_registry_settings
/// \brief GetRegistryVersion, the registry reader for the installed game
///        version number.
///
/// Zero Hour's body from Common/System/registry.cpp, ported through BFME 1,
/// in a unit of its own.

/// \fn void LoadRegistryValues(void)
/// \brief Reads gi.dat once and points each known setting at its value in
///        the loaded block.
///
/// It is reached through the getters (GetRegistryGameName and its
/// siblings), which test and set one shared once-flag before calling it,
/// so it runs at most once per process. That happens early, at the latest
/// when WinMain looks up the splash image: it calls GetRegistryLanguage,
/// whose registry read needs GetRegistryGameRegPath.
///
/// game.dat also holds a small load-once helper that tests and sets the
/// same flag and then jumps here, but a search of game.dat's code finds no
/// call to that helper and no pointer to it (as with the copy helper
/// below, a call through a pointer computed at run time would not show up
/// in such a search).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The path is `<directory>\gi.dat` when the directory-prefix buffer
///   holds a string, else plain `gi.dat` relative to the process's current
///   directory. The buffer starts empty. A small copy helper can set it,
///   but a search of game.dat's code finds no call to that helper and no
///   pointer to it, so as far as the binary shows the path is always plain
///   `gi.dat`. That is not proven: a call through a pointer computed at
///   run time would not show up in such a search.
/// - Before the splash lookup, WinMain makes the executable's folder the
///   working directory unless `lotrsec.big` is visible from the current
///   one, so in that case gi.dat is looked for next to the executable.
///   Whether any getter runs before that point is not traced.
/// - The file is opened in text mode ("rt"), measured by seeking to its
///   end, read whole into one `malloc` block, and checked for the magic and
///   a full-length read (see \ref grp_w2_registry_settings for the format).
/// - Each entry's key and value go to the key dispatcher, which stores the
///   value pointer for the ten known keys.
/// - The file is always closed. The block is never freed, on any path,
///   because the stored pointers point into it.
///
/// Failures are silent: a missing file, a failed seek, a short read or a
/// wrong magic leave every value at its built-in `GI.DAT ERROR` text.
///
/// \warning The format is trusted. A wrong count or a missing NUL makes
///          the scan read past the end of the block, and the result of
///          `malloc` is not checked. The path is built in a fixed
///          512-character buffer with no length check.
/// \par Remastering
/// Because the getters set their flag before calling this, a load that
/// fails is not retried. A port that wants to retry, or to reload
/// settings, has to reset that flag and must free or keep the old block
/// itself.
/// \see GetRegistryGameRegPath, GetRegistryGameName,
///      GetRegistryUserDataLeafName

/// \fn const char *GetRegistrySkuName(void)
/// \brief Returns gi.dat's `SkuName` value, loading the file on the first
///        call to any of the getters.
///
/// This page traces no caller of it, so what the SKU name selects in BFME
/// 2 is not covered here. Zero Hour has no such key; its nearest
/// counterpart is the registry's `SKU` value, which Zero Hour's
/// GetRegistryGameName reads.
/// Whether the two serve the same purpose is not established.
/// \return A pointer into the loaded block, valid until exit, or the text
///         `GI.DAT ERROR` when the file or key is missing. Never null in
///         retail.
/// \see LoadRegistryValues, GetRegistryGameName

/// \fn const char *GetRegistryGameName(void)
/// \brief Returns gi.dat's `GameName` value, the product name the main
///        window's title starts from.
///
/// The title code that initializeAppWindows uses starts from this name
/// and replaces it with the `DisplayName` value stored under
/// `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` at the GetRegistryInstallerRegPath key when that
/// value exists. The window is created with that title. It does not last:
/// GameTextManager::init, which runs later during engine start-up, sets
/// the window's title to the string table's `GUI:FullGameName` entry.
/// \return A pointer into the loaded block, valid until exit, or
///         `GI.DAT ERROR` when the file or key is missing.
/// \par Modding
/// To change the title the player sees, change `GUI:FullGameName` in the
/// string file. GameName and `DisplayName` show only until the text
/// manager has started.
/// \note Zero Hour's GetRegistryGameName is a different function: it
///       returns an AsciiString read from the registry's `SKU` value.
/// \see GetRegistryInstallerRegPath, LoadRegistryValues

/// \fn const char *GetRegistryGameRegPath(void)
/// \brief Returns gi.dat's `GameRegPath` value: the registry key, below the
///        hive root, that BFME 2 keeps its settings under.
///
/// It is the most far-reaching of the gi.dat values. Every
/// GetStringFromRegistry and GetUnsignedIntFromRegistry overload roots its
/// key path at it, trying `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` first and
/// `HKEY_CURRENT_USER` second. All of them except WWDownload's
/// `std::string` GetStringFromRegistry do so through one of the three
/// forms of buildGameRegistryPath (narrow, wide and the `std::string` one
/// in WWDownload), which inserts a backslash before a subkey that does not
/// already start with one. That one reader calls this getter itself and
/// appends the subkey directly, as Zero Hour did. WWDownload's
/// SetStringInRegistry and SetUnsignedIntInRegistry write under the same
/// key through buildGameRegistryPath. Settings that Zero Hour read under
/// its fixed Zero Hour key, such as the language and the version, are read
/// under this key instead.
/// \return A pointer into the loaded block, valid until exit, or
///         `GI.DAT ERROR` when the file or key is missing. In that case
///         GetStringFromRegistry and GetUnsignedIntFromRegistry look under
///         a key of that name and fall back to their defaults.
/// \par Modding
/// Point it at a different key to give a separate install its own
/// language, version, CD key and online settings.
/// \see GetStringFromRegistry, GetRegistryLanguage, GetRegistryVersion

/// \fn const char *GetRegistryInstallerRegPath(void)
/// \brief Returns gi.dat's `InstallerRegPath` value: the installer's
///        registry key, read for the product's display name.
///
/// The use traced here is the main window's first title: the title code
/// opens this key under `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` (not under
/// GetRegistryGameRegPath, and without the `HKEY_CURRENT_USER` fallback)
/// and, when it holds a `DisplayName` value, uses that in place of
/// GetRegistryGameName. The string table later replaces that title (see
/// GetRegistryGameName).
/// \return A pointer into the loaded block, valid until exit, or
///         `GI.DAT ERROR` when the file or key is missing.
/// \see GetRegistryGameName

/// \fn const char *GetRegistryOnlineServer(void)
/// \brief Returns gi.dat's `OnlineServer` value, the default base address
///        for the game's patch and news downloads.
///
/// FormatURLFromRegistry, in WWDownload, appends a `/` to it to form the
/// base URL unless the registry holds a `BaseURL` value, and then builds
/// four URLs from that base: the game patch list (by language and
/// version), the map-pack patch list, `config.txt` and the
/// message-of-the-day file. Zero Hour: the same function, with a fixed EA
/// server address and the SKU compiled into the base URL.
///
/// FormatURLFromRegistry is not its only reader. Code in the online
/// start-up's patch check also calls this getter directly, without
/// consulting `BaseURL`, and hands the value to wininet's
/// `InternetCrackUrlA` to split it as a URL. What it then does with the
/// result is not traced here.
/// \return A pointer into the loaded block, valid until exit, or
///         `GI.DAT ERROR` when the file or key is missing.
/// \par Remastering
/// The address comes from gi.dat, not from the code. The registry's
/// `BaseURL` overrides it only for the URLs FormatURLFromRegistry builds;
/// the patch-check code above still parses gi.dat's `OnlineServer`. A port
/// that revives the patch and news downloads should therefore point
/// `OnlineServer` itself at its own server rather than set `BaseURL`
/// alone.
/// \see FormatURLFromRegistry

/// \fn const char *GetRegistryUserDataLeafName(void)
/// \brief Returns gi.dat's `UserDataLeafName` value, the default name of the
///        folder that holds the player's own files.
///
/// It is only a fallback. getUserDataLeafName (wide) and
/// getUserDataLeafNameAscii (narrow) first ask the registry for a
/// `UserDataLeafName` value under GetRegistryGameRegPath and use this
/// value when none exists. Which BFME 2 code joins the leaf name to a
/// parent folder is not traced here. BFME 1:
/// GlobalData::parseGameDataDefinition appends the leaf name to the user's
/// Application Data folder and creates that folder.
/// \return A pointer into the loaded block, valid until exit, or
///         `GI.DAT ERROR` when the file or key is missing.
/// \see GetRegistryGameRegPath, LoadRegistryValues

/// \fn const char *GetRegistryG1(void)
/// \brief Returns gi.dat's `G1` value, the name of the mutex that marks the
///        launcher as running.
///
/// CopyProtect::isLauncherRunning passes it to `CreateMutexA` and reports
/// the launcher as running when the mutex already exists; it then closes
/// its own handle. BFME 2's WinMain returns at start-up when that check
/// fails. Zero Hour: the same check, with the name compiled in as a GUID
/// string.
/// \return A pointer into the loaded block, valid until exit, or
///         `GI.DAT ERROR` when the file or key is missing.
/// \par Remastering
/// A port that starts the game without the original launcher must create
/// a mutex of this name first, or change the start-up check.
/// \see GetRegistryG3, GetRegistryG4, LoadRegistryValues

/// \fn const char *GetRegistryG3(void)
/// \brief Returns gi.dat's `G3` value, the name of the event the game
///        signals to tell the launcher it has started.
///
/// CopyProtect::notifyLauncher tries for up to a minute to open an event
/// of this name, signals it, and then waits up to ten seconds for the
/// launcher's reply message, which carries a shared-memory handle that it
/// maps. It reports failure when the event never opens or no usable reply
/// arrives, and BFME 2's WinMain then returns. Zero Hour: the same
/// exchange, with the event name compiled in as a GUID string.
/// \return A pointer into the loaded block, valid until exit, or
///         `GI.DAT ERROR` when the file or key is missing.
/// \see GetRegistryG1, GetRegistryG4

/// \fn const char *GetRegistryG4(void)
/// \brief Returns gi.dat's `G4` value, the text the launcher is expected to
///        place in the shared memory.
///
/// CopyProtect::validate accepts the shared memory only when its text
/// starts with `M` and the rest equals this value from its second
/// character on. WinMain passes the result to CopyProtect::setVerified;
/// what depends on that verdict is not covered here. A second check,
/// reached from the game logic's update, first accepts the shared memory
/// when its whole text equals this value, and otherwise applies
/// validate's test. Both checks fail when no shared memory is mapped.
/// Zero Hour's validate compares the whole text with a sentence compiled
/// into the code.
/// \return A pointer into the loaded block, valid until exit, or
///         `GI.DAT ERROR` when the file or key is missing.
/// \see GetRegistryG1, GetRegistryG3

/// \fn AsciiString GetRegistryLanguage(void)
/// \brief Returns the game's language name from the registry's `Language`
///        value, reading it once and caching it for the rest of the run.
///
/// The language name chooses some localised files. Its callers
/// include:
/// - WinMain loads `<language>Splash.jpg`, or `<language>Splash.bmp`
///   failing that, as the splash image;
/// - the game text manager passes it to a format call when it checks
///   whether the string file changed on disk. BFME 2's compiled string
///   file name (`Lotr.csf`) has no placeholder, so the language does not
///   change the path; GameTextManager::init loads that name unformatted.
///   Zero Hour: the name included the language folder;
/// - the online login screen preselects the country whose name matches
///   it, ignoring case and treating `english` or an empty name as United
///   States.
///
/// GameEngine::init also stores it before it creates the file system,
/// which then mounts the language's BIG archives (\ref page_modding_files).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A function-local static string holds the value. It starts as `english`,
/// and a function-local flag makes the registry read happen on the first
/// call only. GetStringFromRegistry reads `Language` under
/// GetRegistryGameRegPath, `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` first. When neither hive
/// has the value the string stays `english`, and it is not looked up again.
/// Each call returns a copy of the cached string.
/// \return The language name, as stored in the registry (the code does not
///         change its case), or `english`.
/// \note Zero Hour's body, unchanged apart from the key path the registry
///       read uses. The cached string lives until exit, when a
///       compiler-generated clean-up destroys it.
/// \see GetRegistryVersion, GetRegistryGameRegPath

/// \fn unsigned int GetRegistryVersion(void)
/// \brief Returns the installed version number from the registry's
///        `Version` value, or 65536 when the value is missing.
///
/// Unlike GetRegistryLanguage it does not cache: every call reads the
/// registry under GetRegistryGameRegPath, `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` first. The
/// caller traced here is PeerThreadClass::Thread_Function, the GameSpy
/// peer thread, which passes the number to the peer SDK's `peerSetTitle`
/// as the title's version when the thread starts, as Zero Hour does.
/// \return The registry's 32-bit `Version` value, or 65536, Zero Hour's
///         default.
/// \note FormatURLFromRegistry and hasWriteAccess do not use this
///       function. They read `Version` themselves through WWDownload's
///       reader; FormatURLFromRegistry's default is 0.
/// \see GetRegistryLanguage, GetRegistryGameRegPath
